# ============================================================================
# Karman — environment configuration EXAMPLE
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Copy this file to `.env` (local) or provide these as real environment
# variables in your deployment target, then replace every placeholder below.
#
# SECURITY: This file is the ONLY env file tracked in version control. It MUST
# contain placeholders only — never real secrets, DSNs, passwords, or tokens.
# `.env`, `.env.production`, and all other `.env.*` files are gitignored.
# See docs/DEPLOYMENT.md for how to generate a strong SESSION_SECRET.
# ============================================================================

# --- App runtime -----------------------------------------------------------
# Node environment. Use "production" for deployed servers, "development"
# locally. Affects cookie `secure` flag and Prisma logging.
# Referenced in: src/server/db.ts, src/server/auth/session.ts
NODE_ENV=production

# --- Database (PostgreSQL) --------------------------------------------------
# Operational runtime database connection string. PLACEHOLDER ONLY — replace
# USER/PASSWORD/HOST and the database name with your real values. Do NOT commit
# a real DSN.
# Referenced in: prisma/schema.prisma (datasource db.url)
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@HOST:5432/karman?schema=public"

# --- Authentication / sessions ---------------------------------------------
# Secret used to HMAC-sign session cookies. PLACEHOLDER ONLY — replace with a
# strong random value (e.g. `openssl rand -base64 48`). The app fails fast if
# this is missing. Never commit a real secret.
# Referenced in: src/server/auth/session.ts
SESSION_SECRET="replace-with-a-strong-random-value"

# --- Public application URL -------------------------------------------------
# Absolute base URL of the deployed app; used for metadata / Open Graph URLs.
# Referenced in: src/app/layout.tsx (metadataBase)
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL="https://your-domain.example"

# ============================================================================
# OPTIONAL — Seed routine (prisma/seed.ts; `npm run seed`)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The seed creates exactly ONE initial SYSTEM_ADMIN user. All three are
# optional and have safe fallbacks:
#   - SEED_ADMIN_EMAIL    defaults to "admin@karman.local"
#   - SEED_ADMIN_NAME     defaults to a Persian "system administrator" label
#   - SEED_ADMIN_PASSWORD if omitted, a strong random password is generated and
#                         printed ONCE to stdout. Set it explicitly for
#                         non-interactive deploys. PLACEHOLDER ONLY below.
# ============================================================================
# SEED_ADMIN_EMAIL="admin@your-domain.example"
# SEED_ADMIN_NAME="System Administrator"
# SEED_ADMIN_PASSWORD="replace-with-a-strong-initial-admin-password"

# ============================================================================
# OPTIONAL — Test-only
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Set RUN_DB_TESTS=1 to additionally run DB-backed integration tests
# (`*.int.test.ts`) against a real PostgreSQL pointed to by DATABASE_URL. When
# unset, `vitest run` stays DB-free and fast.
# Referenced in: vitest.config.ts
# RUN_DB_TESTS=1

# ============================================================================
# OPTIONAL — Build-time asset extraction (local tooling only)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Password for the licensed IRANYekanX font archive, used only by the local
# font-extraction script (`npm run extract:fonts`). Not needed at runtime.
# Referenced in: scripts/extract-fonts.ts
# FONT_RAR_PASSWORD="rar-archive-password-if-applicable"

# ============================================================================
# NOTE on PORT / HOSTNAME
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The Karman application code does NOT read PORT or HOSTNAME directly. `next
# start` honors the standard PORT env var (default 3000) and the `-p`/`-H`
# flags if you need to bind a specific port/host at the process level; set them
# in your process manager / container runtime rather than here.
# ============================================================================
